  abstract={How do action budgets shape vision-language navigation (VLN) exploration, and how does the source of supervision affect learned search? We study budget-conditioned object search: finding as many distinct instances of a requested category as possible within a budget, with discovery defined geometrically by camera field of view and proximity. Distinct discoveries provide a denser evaluation signal than single-target success. We compare two supervision schemes: an object-level Bellman expert routes among targets with the full map and locations, while a room-level Bellman expert represents unexplored rooms through semantic estimates and cross-scene priors, leaving their unseen contents unknown. Both optimize budgeted Bellman objectives within their scene abstractions. We distill them into Uni-NaVid under matched training conditions and also evaluate greedy-frontier supervision on held-out HM3D scenes. Changing only the stated budget changes learned trajectories. Despite higher object-level expert return, room-level supervision achieves the highest mean collection among evaluated VLNs at every tested budget. At 2000 steps, it finds 72.7% of reachable targets, versus 68.5% under greedy-frontier supervision, 65.5% under object-level supervision, and 44.8% for the pretrained VLN. These results show that expert-return rankings need not predict supervision quality for VLN exploration and motivate accounting for budget and expert search behavior when collecting demonstrations.},
}
